Literals
********

.[perex]
The `literals` section decides how values are written directly in the code: arrays as `[]` instead of `array()`, the quotes around strings, digit separators like `1_000_000`, a heredoc without interpolation as a nowdoc, two strings joined with a dot on one line, and a class name in a string.

The key `quotes` applies only to a plain string, that is, a string without an interpolated variable, without an escape sequence that only double quotes understand, and without a quote of the target kind inside. For all other strings, the kind of quotes matters, and they stay as they are.

```neon
literals:
	arraySyntax:
		array: short
	quotes: single
	digitSeparator:
		integer: required
	digitSeparatorMinDigits:
		integer: 7
	concatenatedLiterals: forbidden
```

```php .[before]
$colors = array("red", "green");  // The array must be written `[…]` instead of `array(…)`. // A plain string must be written with single quotes. // A plain string must be written with single quotes.
$limit = 1000000;  // The number `1000000` must be written `1_000_000`.
$query = 'SELECT * ' . 'FROM users';  // Useless concatenation of two string literals, because they make one literal.
$greeting = "Hello $name";
```

```php .[after]
$colors = ['red', 'green'];
$limit = 1_000_000;
$query = 'SELECT * FROM users';
$greeting = "Hello $name";
```

The string with a variable stays in double quotes, because they change its meaning.


literals.arraySyntax.array
==========================

The syntax of an array.

- `short`: `[1, 2]`, never `array(1, 2)`
- `long`: `array(1, 2)`, never `[1, 2]`
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `short`, psr12 `keep`, nette `short`, symfony `short`, laravel `short` · covers `array_syntax`, `Generic.Arrays.DisallowLongArraySyntax`, `Generic.Arrays.DisallowShortArraySyntax` .[decision-facts]


literals.arraySyntax.list
=========================

The syntax of a destructuring.

- `short`: `[$a, $b] =`, never `list($a, $b) =`
- `long`: `list($a, $b) =`, never `[$a, $b] =`
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `short`, symfony `keep`, laravel `short` · covers `list_syntax`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.PHP.ShortList` .[decision-facts]


literals.classNameInString
==========================

`'Acme\Shop\Order'` is `Order::class`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · needs types · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `class_keyword` .[decision-facts]

A class name in a string is rewritten to `::class` only when a class, interface or enum of that name really exists in the project; DressCode finds that out from [types from PHPStan |/types]. The name of a trait is not rewritten, and neither is a string with a leading backslash (`'\Acme\Shop\Order'`), a backslash that a class name in a string does not need. A class declared in a `.phpt` test counts only in that file, because no autoloader loads such a test. According to its own documentation, the PHP CS Fixer rule `class_keyword` cannot tell this, because with tokens alone it does not know which classes the project has.


literals.implicitBackslash
==========================

A backslash of a string that escapes nothing, written `\\` instead.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `escape_implicit_backslashes`, `string_implicit_backslashes` .[decision-facts]


literals.heredocWithoutInterpolation
====================================

A heredoc with nothing to interpolate, which is written as a nowdoc.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `forbidden`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `forbidden` · covers `heredoc_to_nowdoc`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.PHP.RequireNowdoc` .[decision-facts]


literals.digitSeparator.integer
===============================

The separator grouping by three the digits of a decimal integer, or of the integer part of a decimal number, `1_000`; a number grouped already stays.

- `required`: always there
- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `required`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `PhpCsFixerCustomFixers/numeric_literal_separator`, `numeric_literal_separator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Numbers.DisallowNumericLiteralSeparator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Numbers.RequireNumericLiteralSeparator` .[decision-facts]


literals.digitSeparator.fraction
================================

The separator grouping by three the digits of the fraction of a decimal number, `0.000_1`.

- `required`: always there
- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `required`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `PhpCsFixerCustomFixers/numeric_literal_separator`, `numeric_literal_separator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Numbers.DisallowNumericLiteralSeparator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Numbers.RequireNumericLiteralSeparator` .[decision-facts]


literals.digitSeparatorMinDigits.integer
========================================

The number of digits from which `literals.digitSeparator.integer` groups them.

- a number from 1

Default `4` · standards: perCs `4`, psr12 `4`, nette `7`, symfony `4`, laravel `4` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Numbers.RequireNumericLiteralSeparator` .[decision-facts]


literals.digitSeparatorMinDigits.fraction
=========================================

The number of digits from which `literals.digitSeparator.fraction` groups them.

- a number from 1

Default `4` · standards: perCs `4`, psr12 `4`, nette `20`, symfony `4`, laravel `4` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Numbers.RequireNumericLiteralSeparator` .[decision-facts]


literals.quotes
===============

The quotes of a plain string, one without interpolation, without an escape sequence only double quotes know and without a quote of the wanted kind inside.

- `single`: `'text'` where double quotes gain nothing
- `double`: `"text"` for every plain string, the backslashes of a single-quoted one escaped
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `single`, symfony `single`, laravel `single` · covers `single_quote`, `Squiz.Strings.DoubleQuoteUsage` .[decision-facts]


literals.concatenatedLiterals
=============================

Two string literals concatenated on one line, which are one literal, `'ab'` for `'a' . 'b'`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `PhpCsFixerCustomFixers/no_superfluous_concatenation`, `no_useless_concat_operator`, `Generic.Strings.UnnecessaryStringConcat` .[decision-facts]


literals.concatenatedLiteralsOverLines
======================================

Whether two string literals concatenated over a line break are one literal too.

- `yes` or `no`

Default `no` · standards: perCs `no`, psr12 `no`, nette `no`, symfony `no`, laravel `no` · covers `Generic.Strings.UnnecessaryStringConcat` .[decision-facts]


literals.emptyStringConcatenation
=================================

The concatenation with an empty string, which is a cast or nothing, `(string) $x` for `$x . ''`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `PhpCsFixerCustomFixers/no_superfluous_concatenation`, `Generic.Strings.UnnecessaryStringConcat` .[decision-facts]
